    17	## 2. Summary verdict
    18	
    19	The paper is honest and its numbers match its outputs. Its conclusions are narrower than its framing. The main result is a null: R3 and a history-based comparator differ by 0.021 in attacker gain. The "equivalent" label comes from a wide margin, and three things can change it: search error as large as the margin, variation between markets, and a comparator that was not selected on gain. The two Q1 "families" are mostly one test. The model has no honest churn, so identity age labels the attacker almost perfectly. The theory is correct but thin. The paper reads as an audit record, not as a finding.
    20	
    21	Scores: **WEIS 2/5, AAMAS 2/5, EC 1/5** (details in section 8).
